# Compiled binaries
scripts/optimizations/copy_to_hugetlbfs

# Model weights staged inside the worktree for agent-sandbox sessions. The
# sandbox mounts only the worktree, so a checkpoint has to live here to be
# reachable; multi-GB binaries must never reach a commit.
# Leading slash matters: a bare `models/` also matches training/models/, which is
# source.
/models/
*.gguf
*.safetensors
# The committed test fixtures are the deliberate exception: they are kilobytes,
# they are the golden inputs the runtime's tests load, and rust-harness/fixtures
# is where regenerating them writes. Without these negations `git add` would need
# -f every time the fixture is regenerated.
#
# The safetensors negation is not a convenience. Until it existed the tiny
# checkpoints were ignored while their config.json was tracked, so a fresh clone
# carried half a fixture and `make quality` failed three cpu_reference tests with
# "missing model.safetensors" until someone happened to run `tiny-model` by hand.
# The generator is still the source of truth -- cpu_reference regenerates and
# byte-compares, so a stale committed fixture fails the gate -- but the gate now
# passes on a clone, which is the whole point of a gate.
#
# It also makes the transformers-authored fixture possible at all. That one is
# built by torch and cannot be regenerated in the sandbox, so committing its
# weights is the only way the sandbox can check anything against it.
!rust-harness/fixtures/*.gguf
!rust-harness/fixtures/**/*.safetensors

# Claude Code per-session runtime state. The lock records a live sessionId, pid,
# and acquisition time; it is regenerated every session and must never be
# committed. settings.local.json is per-checkout permission state, accreted by
# approving prompts with "don't ask again": the host copy and every sandbox
# worktree copy answer different questions, so tracking it would merge two
# policies into one that nobody decided. Standing policy belongs in
# .claude/settings.json, which is tracked. Everything else under .claude/ --
# agents, commands, skills, and agent-memory -- is version-controlled on purpose.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/settings.local.json

# Repo-local agent and evaluation workflow state, not run data -- both are tied to a
# checkout rather than to the workstation, which is why they are not under /mnt/ai-data:
#   logs/sessions/  exported agent transcripts (make agent-export, SESSIONS_DIR)
#   logs/eval/      serve-eval reports (training/commands/serve_eval.py, anchored to REPO_ROOT)
# Leading slash matters: a bare `logs/` would also match docs/ prose paths and interp/logs.
/logs/

# Rust harness session transcripts: the container's working directory, where it writes
# session.jsonl and probe-flags.jsonl on every run (docker-compose.rust-harness.yaml:42).
# Pre-created user-owned by make/harness.mk so the container's ${UID} can write into it.
/sessions/

# Temporary files
*.tmp
*.bak
CLEANUP_PLAN.md

# Agent tooling state (token usage ledger written by usage.ts)
.agent-state/

